𝗔𝗪𝗦 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“How would you design an architecture where users can schedule file uploads to S3 using AWS Lambda?”

One of the most interesting AWS system design questions I was asked.

At first, it sounded simple:

“Users should be able to schedule uploads to S3 at a future time.”

But the deeper we discussed it, the more it became a real architecture conversation.

Here’s how I approached it 👇

🧠 𝗥𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗶𝗿𝗲𝗺𝗲𝗻𝘁

The system should allow users to:

✅ Upload a file
✅ Choose a future upload time
✅ Automatically upload/store the file in S3
✅ Handle retries/failures
✅ Scale for many users

🏗️ 𝗔𝗿𝗰𝗵𝗶𝘁𝗲𝗰𝘁𝘂𝗿𝗲

API Gateway → Lambda → DynamoDB → EventBridge Scheduler → Lambda → S3

𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟭 — 𝗨𝘀𝗲𝗿 𝗥𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁

API Gateway triggers Lambda.

Lambda validates:
• file type
• upload timestamp
• authentication
• request limits

📦 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟮 — 𝗦𝘁𝗼𝗿𝗲 𝗠𝗲𝘁𝗮𝗱𝗮𝘁𝗮

Store schedule details in DynamoDB:

{
 "jobId": "123",
 "uploadTime": "2026-05-20T10:00:00Z",
 "status": "PENDING"
}


Why DynamoDB?

✅ Serverless
✅ Highly scalable
✅ Low latency

⏰ 𝗦𝘁𝗲𝗽 𝟯 — 𝗦𝗰𝗵𝗲𝗱𝘂𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴

Instead of polling continuously:

EventBridge Scheduler → Lambda → S3

This:
✅ reduces compute overhead
✅ scales efficiently
✅ keeps architecture serverless

☁️ 𝗦𝟯 𝗕𝗲𝘀𝘁 𝗣𝗿𝗮𝗰𝘁𝗶𝗰𝗲𝘀

✅ Versioning
✅ Lifecycle policies
✅ SSE-KMS encryption
✅ Access logging

🚨 𝗧𝗵𝗲𝗻 𝘁𝗵𝗲 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄𝗲𝗿 𝗔𝘀𝗸𝗲𝗱:

“What are the limitations of Lambda?”

This is where interviews become interesting.

Because designing architecture is easy.

Designing around limitations shows experience.

⚠️ 𝗟𝗮𝗺𝗯𝗱𝗮 𝗟𝗶𝗺𝗶𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻𝘀

⏱️ 15-minute timeout
→ Use Step Functions / ECS Fargate

💾 Limited `/tmp` storage
→ Stream directly to S3

🚀 Cold starts
→ Provisioned concurrency

📈 Concurrency limits
→ EventBridge → SQS → Lambda → S3

📦 Large package size
→ Lambda Layers / containers

🔁 Retry storms
→ DLQ + idempotent processing

🔐 𝗦𝗲𝗰𝘂𝗿𝗶𝘁𝘆

✅ IAM least privilege
✅ Pre-signed URLs
✅ Encryption at rest + transit
✅ CloudTrail auditing

💡 𝗙𝗶𝗻𝗮𝗹 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

AWS interviews are rarely about memorizing services.

They test:
• scaling decisions
• failure handling
• tradeoff understanding
• architectural thinking

Because real cloud engineering starts when systems fail at scale.
